  "scriptId": "sim-01-order-of-operations-bodmas",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Order of Operations: The BODMAS Rule",
    "description": "Apply the correct sequence of operations (of, division, multiplication, addition, subtraction) so an expression has exactly one value.",
    "teachingApproach": "Open with two students getting two answers to one expression, then reveal BODMAS as the rule that picks the right one. Move from single-step order questions to chained division, negative values, and 'of' blocks, always showing one change per line and placing a fresh matching question after each worked example. Close with missing-number equations that reuse BODMAS in reverse.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 4, Simplification. Approved scope: Teach the BODMAS order as the reason an expression like 6 + 4 × 5 has only one value. Cover multiplication and division before addition and subtraction, 'of' binding tighter than division, and left-to-right evaluation within the same level, including chained divisions such as 1888 ÷ 32 ÷ 8 where doing 32 ÷ 8 first is the classic trap. Include expressions whose value is negative, and equation forms such as 13 × 252 ÷ 42 + 170 = ? + 47 and 853 + ? ÷ 17 = 1000 where the learner evaluates the known side first. Name the BODMAS letters, show each step on its own line, and check answers by redoing the operations in order. Prepare learners for exam phrasing such as 'What will come in place of the question mark' and options that include the left-to-right error."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn the rule that decides every simplification question.\n\nBy the end you will look at a line like 4848 ÷ 24 × 11 − 222 and know exactly which step comes first, which comes last, and which printed option is the trap.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn the one rule that decides every simplification question.\n\nTwo students simplify 6 + 4 × 5.\n\nMeena adds first: 10 × 5 = 50.\nRahul multiplies first: 6 + 20 = 26.\n\nOnly one of them can be right.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Mathematics has a fixed order of operations, called BODMAS:\n\nB → Brackets\nO → Of\nD → Division\nM → Multiplication\nA → Addition\nS → Subtraction\n\nSo multiplication happens before addition.\n\n6 + 4 × 5\n= 6 + 20\n= 26\n\nRahul is right. Meena's 50 is the classic trap answer.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step2": {
      "id": "teach_1_step2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Read BODMAS as a queue. Division and multiplication stand ahead of addition and subtraction.\n\nSo in any expression:\nfirst clear brackets and 'of',\nthen ÷ and ×,\nand only then + and −.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
