    "teach_6": {
      "id": "teach_6",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "One letter of BODMAS is easy to forget: O stands for 'of'.\n\n'Of' means multiplication, but it sits ahead of ÷ and ×.\n\n36 ÷ 6 of 2 + 3\n\nFirst: 6 of 2 = 6 × 2 = 12\nThen: 36 ÷ 12 = 3\nLast: 3 + 3 = 6",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_6_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_6_step": {
      "id": "teach_6_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If you ignored 'of' and went left to right, you would get 36 ÷ 6 = 6, then 6 × 2 = 12, then 12 + 3 = 15.\n\nThat is exactly the mistake the exam is testing.\n\nRule of thumb: read 'of' as glued to its number — 6 of 2 is one package, value 12.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_6"
        }
      ]
    },
